welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Steven Wilkinson vanilla Connie  professor of India and South Asian  Studies and professor of political  science and international affairs at  Yale University much of Professor  Wilkinson's work focuses on India and  ethnic violence his book votes and  violence electoral competition and  ethnic riots in India was co-winner of  the American political science  associations 2005 Woodrow Wilson prize  for the best book published in  government politics and International  Affairs today we'll talk with him about  his current book project on colonization  democracy and conflict  welcome professor Wilkinson Thank You  Milo let's begin with an overview of  your book and the questions that are at  its foundation so the main question I  look at is why of colonies done so  differently since independence  especially why I have colonies that seem  to be superficially similar there are  French colonies for instance or British  colonies or Portuguese why given these  similarities do they seem to have done  so differently in terms of their levels  of democracy their levels of ethnic and  other kinds of conflict or their levels  of governance how did you come to write  this book what was your inspiration so I  think part of it was my fascination by  the question in India why India and  Pakistan have done so differently since  independence India has had a much  happier experience with democracy with  its management of ethnic conflict with  its level of State strengthen governance  than Pakistan even though both were part  of the same country the British colony  of India prior to 1947 so part of the  inspiration is just how do you  understand things like that these  differences given similarities within  the same Empire I think the second  aspect is related to my interest in  ethnic conflict I'd want to be  understood in working at which kind of  systems can moderate ethnic  phlex why some countries seem to have  higher levels of violence than others  and obviously in answering that question  it's important to get a sense of where  colonies start some countries become  independent with much higher levels of  ethnic conflict ethnic imbalance than  others and so you need to get a good  sense of the colonial legacy if you're  going to answer that kind of question  and then the third issue I think is that  the way in which political scientists  and economists have tended to look at  these questions have used very blunt  indicators of the colonial experience so  for instance they use were you French or  not French  or were you British or not British and  the fact is that there were enormous  difference is not just between empires  but also within empires so for instance  in the British Empire at the point of  Independence some colonies have 250 or  300 years of experience running  democratic institutions and other or at  least electoral institutions if not for  them fully democratic and other  countries have had essentially no  experience with running their democratic  institutions some countries are highly  ethnically imbalanced others aren't so  there's enormous variation across these  countries that I didn't really see out  there in the existing work that was  being done and so I wanted to develop  better indicators of those kinds of okay  so the data collection and its analysis  play a very important role in your book  tell us about it yeah so in in data most  of the interesting questions I think in  almost any field it's very difficult to  answer them with data that are just off  the shelf right there is no free lunch  and data analysis and so if you're  interested in in getting better answers  you often have to just go out and do the  hard work of gathering the data yourself  and I'm interested really in the three  kinds of aspects of the colonial  experience that I think are most  important one is looking at the legacy  in terms of party development on  electoral institutions during the  colonial period if you compare different  states how many years of electoral  institutions have they had what kind of  party systems have they had how much  experience with alternation of  government those kinds of things the  second thing is levels of he  Capital Development what it's done are  locals running the state at the time of  Independence in some states like the  Belgian Congo it's a it's mainly  Belgians who are running all the higher  parts of the state and there isn't much  training and not much capacity  underneath them whereas in other states  like India for instance you have the  very top reaches of the colonial civil  service are 50% perhaps or even more  than 50% people from the local community  at the time of colonization so there's a  very different capacity there that  doesn't mean that they were fully  democratic fully free but still you have  different levels of of inheritance and  then the third kind of thing that I'm  interested in is ethnic imbalance some  states were ethnically much more  imbalanced than others at the point of  Independence and you could be doing very  well in terms of your level of democracy  or your level of human capacity training  at the point of Independence but if all  those goodies are going to members of  one group then you're in for trouble and  that nowhere is the story between India  and Pakistan okay so how did you  actually get the data it seems like a  quite a bit of data would be required  yes so secondary sources  archival sources basically going through  with a team of a search assistance there  must have been probably 10 research  assistants of various points in the  project together with with me working  reading through lots of secondary  sources weeding through lots of archival  sources on 144 different countries you  can't look at every aspect of those  companies but you can at least get some  sense of how they vary over the most  important kinds of variables so why is  it important to look at colonial  legacies um  I think the most important reason is  that they still have a substantial  impact on the companies today in many  cases so to come back to the Pakistani  case Pakistan's army today is very much  a product of the colonial period in  terms of its regimental composition in  terms of its ethnic balance you cannot  understand the role of the army in  Pakistan and  composition of the Pakistani army unless  you understand the colonial inheritance  nor can you understand why Pakistan has  had such a bad experience with democracy  compared to India so in that way it  fundamentally shapes many countries I  think the second main reason is because  even if you're even if you don't care  about colonialism all you're interested  in is economic growth or institutions or  democracy even if you care nothing about  colonialism the fact is that the  colonial legacy influences your analyses  or ought to influence your analyses of  how all these other things work and if  you don't get a good sense if you're  doing a large cross-national analysis of  what the colonial legacy was it's very  difficult to come up with good  explanations there that are going to be  accurate on how these other things work  democracy institutions unless you get a  good sense of the colonial period so in  intercourse is an ongoing book progress  that's not it's not quite finished yet  but one of the big ones and this is in a  co-author of paper with Massimo Anuradha  who's from the Kona University in Italy  is the the longer the electoral  experience before independence even if  it's not fully a free electoral  experience and in the way that we would  think of free democratic institutions  the better you're going to do in terms  of your likelihood of being democratic  after independence so the stock of your  democracy at the point of Independence  makes a big difference to how you're  going to do subsequently one of the  second things is just the ethnic  imbalances a point of independents make  a huge difference to your likelihood of  a conflict so you could be doing really  well in terms of your electoral  inheritance but if your ethnic lis very  imbalanced in the military in the civil  service or in who gets access to  politics you're headed for trouble about  the point of Independence and then the  third thing is that the human capital  experience also makes a difference so  just to give you one instance it's  impossible I think to understand  the Belgian Congo which is not a state  with Congo and how it's done since  independence without realizing that a  year before independence in the Belgian  Congo they had 5000 senior civil  servants not one of whom was Congolese  they had an army with a thousand  officers not one of whom was Congolese  they had 14 to 20 depending on which  estimate you read university graduates  in the entire country of 14 million  people and basically just a few hundred  high school graduates as well this is a  country that in all respects is not  prepared for independence other  countries India has a hundred thousand  students in university in the 1930s you  know they have much better legacies in  terms of where they are at the point of  Independence and it's impossible to  understand how the countries do unless  you got a sense of where their starting  points are at a point with independence  and final question what was the greatest  surprise to you and doing the research  um I think there were several surprises  one was that many colonies didn't  actually make money I went into this  thinking that many of the colonies were  pure exploitative colonies and I think  they were exploited too but the fact of  the matter is that a lot of them didn't  make money the Germans for instance  heavily subsidized their colonies that  was one surprise I think another  surprise was the way in which countries  are not prisoners of the past that good  policy decisions have been able to turn  things around in many countries Tanzania  Singapore inherited very ethnically  imbalanced armies but managed to turn  the situations around and more generally  the importance of the individual I went  into this thinking that a lot of the  colonial legacies were going to explain  a very large part of how countries had  done after independence and that I think  they do but it's also important to  realize that the difference between  having a Jinnah and Pakistan and an  arrow in India after 1947 also makes a  difference on leadership problems in the  colonial period in post-colonial period  justice in our own era play a large part  in explaining the kind of outcomes you  get go to good leadership masters  good thank you so much for being here  with us today and sharing some of your  research well thanks for us for more  information about Professor Wilkinson  and his work please visit our website at  Yale edu backslash Macmillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

